/* Legacy theme for the server-select dialog (CServerSelWin). A fresh RmlUi layout, not a pixel
   port of the old CWinEx/CButton/CGaugeBar screen -- there's no legacy geometry left to match (see
   ui-target-architecture.md's note on why CWinEx's asset-derived panel height couldn't safely be
   preserved when this window's native backdrop was retired). The load gauge is a flat color fill,
   matching component-catalog.md's guidance not to invent a "reusable primitive" ahead of a second
   real need.

   #panel itself is intentionally invisible (no background/border) -- the real legacy screen has
   no panel chrome at all, just buttons and text floating over the login art.

   Two always-visible columns laid out by real flexbox (#columns/#group_col/#server_col below),
   not the old absolutely-positioned flyout that ServerSelWin.cpp used to dock beside whichever
   group button was clicked via C++-computed pixel offsets (kGroupBtnRowHeight and friends -- see
   ServerSelWin.h's own header comment). #panel/#pvp_notice/#desc_panel are plain normal-flow block
   content now too, not position:absolute with hand-picked top offsets -- #panel's height is just
   whatever its real children need, no separate oversized worst-case reservation to center
   against. This box is what .center-both actually centers; ServerSelWin.h's kPanelWidth/
   kPanelHeight is a separate, deliberately generous rect used only for the native click-gate
   (UpdateMouseEvent()), not this box's own size. */

/* No explicit position here -- .center-both (base.rcss) supplies position:absolute plus the
   left/top/transform centering math, same as sys_menu.rcss/msg_win.rcss's own #panel. Redeclaring
   position on this ID rule too is harmless in standard CSS cascade terms, but every other
   self-centering window here leaves it out, so this matches that precedent exactly rather than
   introducing an unexplained difference. */
#panel {
	width: 420px;
	font-family: "Liberation Sans";
	font-size: 12px;
	color: white;
	padding: 10px;
}

/* height:300px is fixed, not auto -- #panel uses .center-both, which recenters based on #panel's
   OWN CURRENT size every time it changes; an auto height here let #columns grow the instant the
   server column populated after a group click, visibly shifting the whole panel (including the
   just-clicked group button) up/down as .center-both recentered around the new, taller box. A
   fixed height means #panel's total size never changes across the empty/populated/hidden states,
   so nothing ever needs to recenter. Each column below still scrolls internally past this height
   via its own overflow:auto -- this box just never resizes around them. */
#columns {
	display: flex;
	flex-direction: row;
	align-items: flex-start;
	gap: 18px;
	height: 300px;
}

/* height (not max-height) matches #columns' own fixed height above, for the same reason -- a
   shorter real group/server list just centers within this fixed box (justify-content:center)
   rather than shrinking it. overflow:auto still scrolls a list taller than this box. */
#group_col {
	display: flex;
	flex-direction: column;
	justify-content: center;
	gap: 6px;
	width: 140px;
	height: 300px;
	overflow: hidden auto;
}
#server_col {
	display: flex;
	flex-direction: column;
	justify-content: center;
	gap: 6px;
	width: 220px;
	height: 300px;
	overflow: hidden auto;
}

/* Sprite provides the border/frame (ninepatch, base.rcss), stretched from its native 108x30 rect;
   a translucent tint decorator layered on top (RmlUi paints the first-listed decorator in a
   comma-separated list frontmost, later ones behind) gives the glassy body read instead of the
   sprite's own opaque fill.

   Height (32px, no padding/border-width to add to it) matches .server-row's real on-screen height
   below (25px declared + its own 3px+4px padding = 32px -- see that rule's comment for why its
   declared height isn't literally 32 too). width is 140px, not the old two-column layout's 108px
   -- #group_col is now the only column on its side, so there's no second 108px column to leave
   room for. No margin-bottom here -- #group_col's own `gap` above handles row spacing now. */
.group-btn {
	width: 140px;
	height: 32px;
	text-align: center;
	vertical-align: middle;
	line-height: 32px;
	cursor: pointer;
	pointer-events: auto;
	decorator: linear-gradient(to bottom, rgba(0, 0, 0, 140), rgba(0, 0, 0, 140)), ninepatch(legacy-btn-idle, legacy-btn-idle-inner);
	color: #d8d8d8;
}
.group-btn:hover {
	decorator: linear-gradient(to bottom, rgba(40, 40, 40, 150), rgba(40, 40, 40, 150)), ninepatch(legacy-btn-hover, legacy-btn-hover-inner);
}
.group-btn.checked {
	decorator: linear-gradient(to bottom, rgba(60, 50, 20, 150), rgba(60, 50, 20, 150)), ninepatch(legacy-btn-active, legacy-btn-active-inner);
	color: #ffffff;
}

/* Same construction as .group-btn above -- sprite ninepatch for the border/frame, a translucent
   tint decorator layered in front for the glassy body, so both buttons share one look. No CSS
   `border` property here: this element has a child (.server-gauge-slot below), and a border-width
   property on a bordered element with a child does not render as a closed rectangle in this RmlUi
   build. The ninepatch decorator paints via a separate code path and isn't affected.

   Width (200px) isn't the same as .group-btn's 140px -- the label (s.label, e.g.
   "Valhalla-1(Gold) Connect") needs this much room to fit on one line; ninepatch (unlike the plain
   image() decorator, which only renders cleanly at the sprite's native 108x30 size) stretches to
   this wider box without distortion.

   height is 25px, not .group-btn's 32px -- `height` sets content size only, and padding is added
   on top of it. 25 + this row's own 3px+4px padding = 32, matching .group-btn's real on-screen
   height (no border-width here to add on top, since the ninepatch decorator paints its own frame
   without a separate border box).

   width is a literal px value (200 = #server_col's 220px minus this row's own 10px+10px padding).
   .server-gauge-slot has neither padding nor border, so its width is the direct target (180px),
   inset by a literal 10px left margin to center it within .server-row's own 200px content width.
   No margin-bottom here -- #server_col's own `gap` handles row spacing now. */
.server-row {
	display: block;
	width: 200px;
	height: 25px;
	padding: 3px 10px 4px;
	text-align: center;
	line-height: 14px;
	white-space: nowrap;
	cursor: pointer;
	pointer-events: auto;
	decorator: linear-gradient(to bottom, rgba(0, 0, 0, 140), rgba(0, 0, 0, 140)), ninepatch(legacy-btn-idle, legacy-btn-idle-inner);
	color: #f0d74d;
}
.server-row:hover {
	decorator: linear-gradient(to bottom, rgba(40, 40, 40, 150), rgba(40, 40, 40, 150)), ninepatch(legacy-btn-hover, legacy-btn-hover-inner);
}
.server-row:active {
	transform: translateY(1px);
}
.server-row.gray {
	color: #b0b0b0;
}
.server-row.orange {
	color: #ff9c3c;
}

/* Plain dark recessed fill, not a `repeating-linear-gradient` tick-mark decorator -- this engine
   doesn't support repeating gradients (see modern-theme-visual-direction.md's compatibility
   table), so the old decorator silently rendered as nothing rather than the intended dashed
   track. */
.server-gauge-slot {
	display: block;
	width: 180px;
	height: 5px;
	margin: 4px 0 0 10px;
	background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 160);
}
.server-gauge-fill {
	display: block;
	height: 100%;
	background-color: #00bd8b;
}

/* Normal block flow below #columns now, not position:absolute with a hand-picked top offset. */
#pvp_notice {
	margin-top: 16px;
	width: 100%;
	color: #ffcc66;
	font-size: 11px;
}

#desc_panel {
	margin-top: 10px;
	width: 100%;
	color: #d8d8d8;
	white-space: normal;
}
